# Commit when done

When a discrete piece of work is finished, commit it. Do not wait for
"commit" or "commit and push".

## When

- A feature, fix, or UI change is working and you would otherwise stop
- You finished the user's request (or a self-contained slice of it)
- One concern is complete even if more work remains

## How

- Follow `.cursor/rules/git-commit-messages.mdc`
- One concern per commit
- Stage only the files for that concern
- Do not commit secrets, `.env`, or unrelated dirty files
- Push after the commit when `origin` already tracks this branch

## Do not

- Commit broken or speculative WIP just to "save progress"
- Bundle unrelated edits into one commit
- Ask whether to commit after finishing the work
